course: dictionary
lesson: 10820
title: Informal words and short forms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are informal: people use them in friendly talk, not in formal writing or exams. «Eat your veggies!» is something a parent might say at dinner. Veggies is a short form of vegetables, so the sentence means eat your vegetables.
explain: One short word can have more than one meaning. Veggie can be a noun meaning a vegetable, or a noun meaning a vegetarian, someone who does not eat meat. In the UK and Australia, «I'm veggie» means «I'm vegetarian». The words around it tell you which meaning fits.
explain: Veggie can also be an adjective, describing a thing. In «a veggie burger», veggie tells you what kind of burger it is: one without meat. Check the words around it: if there is a noun next to it, like burger, it is probably working as an adjective.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Informal words are used in friendly talk, not in formal writing.
fact: «Veggie» is informal: it is a short form of vegetable, and also of vegetarian.
fact: «Veggie» can be a noun: «Eat your veggies!» means eat your vegetables.
fact: «Veggie» can be an adjective: «a veggie burger» is a burger with no meat.
exercise: «Eat your veggies!» What are veggies in this sentence? | Vegetarians | *Vegetables | Lunch boxes | Hamburgers
exercise: «I'm veggie, so I don't eat meat.» What does veggie mean here? | A green colour | A kind of burger with cheese | A vegetable, like a carrot | *Vegetarian, someone who does not eat meat
exercise: In «a veggie burger», what kind of word is veggie? | *An adjective, describing the burger | A verb, showing an action | A full sentence | A noun, naming the burger
test: Mia's mum says, «Have you eaten your veggies yet?» What does veggies mean? | Hamburgers | Vegetables | *Vegetables, short for the informal word, not meat | Vegetarians who cook at home
test: Ben says, «I'm veggie, so I'll have the pasta.» What does Ben mean? | *He is vegetarian and does not eat meat | He wants to be a farmer | He is a vegetable | He likes to dance
exam: In the UK, a friend says, «I'm veggie.» Which meaning fits best, and how do the other words help? | *Vegetarian, because a friend talking about food and eating shows they do not eat meat | A colour, because veggie is a happy word | A holiday, because it sounds like a party | Vegetable, because it is a green food
